---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Methodology';
const description = 'How World Battery Hub researches, verifies and corrects its battery data — source hierarchy, data verification, standards verification, manufacturer-claim policy, market-data method and correction policy.';
const canonical = '/methodology/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  dateModified="2026-09-18"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Editorial Policy · Last reviewed 2026-09-18</p>
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        This page states how World Battery Hub produces, verifies, corrects and updates its content and data.
        It exists so a reader or an AI can see, before trusting a number, exactly how that number was obtained —
        and what its confidence level is.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> Every claim on this platform is produced by the
        <a href="/about/#research-desk">World Battery Hub Research Desk</a> from public sources, tagged with a
        source, a standard and a confidence level, and corrected on a public schedule when it is wrong. We do
        not fabricate figures, and we do not present a single-author "expert" persona — the desk is a
        collective editorial entity with a stated process, not a person.
      </div>

      <h2>Research methodology</h2>
      <p>
        Content is synthesized from multiple independent sources — including Chinese and English primary
        material — rather than translated from a single source. The method runs in four steps:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Collect</strong> — gather primary material: manufacturer datasheets, standards documents, government and industry data.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-check</strong> — reconcile at least two independent sources for any factual claim; where they agree, record the agreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Label</strong> — attach the source, the test standard and the confidence level to every number.</li>
        <li><strong>Explain</strong> — where sources disagree, state the disagreement and its likely cause instead of silently choosing one number.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
      <p>
        Sources are ranked by independence and authority, in this order:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Primary</strong> — manufacturer documentation, standards bodies (SAE, EN, JIS, IEC, UL, UN), government and regulatory data.</li>
        <li><strong>Secondary</strong> — industry publications, trade organisations and technical reviews.</li>
        <li><strong>Community</strong> — forums and user reports, used only to discover real-world questions, never as evidence.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A claim is only asserted as fact when it can be traced to a primary or a corroborated secondary source.
        Anything below that is stated qualitatively, or marked as unknown.
      </p>

      <h2>Data verification</h2>
      <p>
        Database fields (battery-group dimensions, CCA ranges, chemistry parameters) are written only when a
        source supports them. Where a value is a <strong>typical range</strong>, a
        <strong>standard-defined</strong> limit or a <strong>manufacturer claim</strong>, the field is tagged
        with that <code>value_type</code> so a reader or an AI cannot mistake a representative range for a
        guarantee. A missing value is written as <code>null</code> — never estimated to fill a gap.
      </p>

      <h2>Manufacturer-claim policy</h2>
      <p>
        Manufacturer data (company identity, capacity, certifications) is recorded at the verification level the
        evidence supports — <strong>claimed</strong> until it is independently corroborated, then
        <strong>cross-verified</strong>, then <strong>independently verified</strong>. We do not upgrade a
        manufacturer's own claim to "verified" without independent evidence, and we state plainly when a
        certification has not been independently accredited here.
      </p>

      <h2>Standards verification</h2>
      <p>
        A rating is only meaningful with its standard and test condition. Where we state a CCA or capacity
        value, we state the governing standard (SAE J537, EN 50342, JIS D5301, DIN 43539, IEC 60095, etc.) and
        the test condition. We do not compare values across standards as if they were interchangeable — the
        temperature and cutoff differ, and the difference is stated, not hidden.
      </p>

      <h2>Conflict resolution</h2>
      <p>
        When sources conflict, we do not pick the convenient number. We report the conflict, list the competing
        values with their sources, and state the likely reason for the difference (different year, scope,
        geography, or test method). Where a single value must be given, we give the range and its confidence.
      </p>

      <h2>Market-data method</h2>
      <p>
        Market figures (prices per kWh, GWh production, market size) are especially easy to misread because they
        vary by year, scope, geography, and battery definition (cell vs pack, production vs shipment, revenue vs
        market value). Each market figure is tagged with its <strong>metric, value, year, scope, source, method
        and confidence</strong>. A figure without that frame is not published.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Errors are corrected in place, and material corrections are logged. To report an error, use the contact
        route on the <a href="/about/">About page</a>. A correction does not rewrite history silently — the
        change is recorded with the date, the old value and the new value.
      </p>

      <h2>Update policy</h2>
      <p>
        Pages and database entities carry a <strong>last-reviewed</strong> date. Database entities are revised
        on a schedule and record a revision history where the underlying data changes. Content is not left to
        drift — the review date is part of the page, and a stale page is marked as such rather than presented
        as current.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
      <p>
        The platform has a commercial section (the <a href="/lead-acid/products/">product catalogue</a>) that is
        clearly separated from the editorial and data content. Editorial conclusions, buyer guidance and the
        stance blocks are the Research Desk's own analysis and are labelled as such — they are not paid
        placement, and a commercial relationship does not change a rating, a standard or a confidence level.
      </p>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-18</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
